{:edb/db-before-t 2476 :edb/db-after-t 2477 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535789 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:17:04.994Z" 13194139535789 true] [17592186166632 1100 "The third-order differential equation\n\n$y''' - 3\\sqrt{t}\\, y'' + t^3 y' - 4t^2 y= 0$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = f(t)$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$, $x_2(t) = y'(t)$ and $x_3(t) = y''(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$." 13194139535789 false] [17592186166632 1100 "The third-order differential equation \n\n$$\ny''' - 3\\sqrt{t}\\, y'' + t^3 y' - 4t^2 y= 0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t),$ $x_2(t) = y'(t)$ and $x_3(t) = y''(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" 13194139535789 true] [17592186166632 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= y \\\\\nx_{2} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{3} &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\nx_{1}^{′} = y^{′}; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}; x_{3}^{′} = y^{‴}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3},; x_{3}^{′} = y^{‴}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2$, and $x_3$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\,y'' - t^3 y' + 4t^2 y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\, x_3 - t^3 x_2 + 4t^2 x_1\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = 4t^{2}x_{1} - t^{3}x_{2} + 3\\sqrt{t}x_{3}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = 4t^2 x_1 - t^3 x_2 + 3\\sqrt{t} \\,x_3\n$$" 13194139535789 false] [17592186166632 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2,$ and $x_3.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\,y'' - t^3 y' + 4t^2 y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' = 3\\sqrt{t}\\, x_3 - t^3 x_2 + 4t^2 x_1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=4{t}^{2}\\,{x}_{1}-{t}^{3}{x}_{2}+3\\sqrt{t}\\,{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = 4t^2 x_1 - t^3 x_2 + 3\\sqrt{t} \\,x_3.$" 13194139535789 true] [17592186166633 1100 "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny''  + y' - 6y= 0 , \\quad y(0)= 1, \\quad y'(0)= 4\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t),$ $x_2(t) = y'(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" 13194139535789 true] [17592186166633 1100 "The second-order initial value problem\n$y'' + y' - 6y= 0, \\quad y(0)= 1, \\quad y'(0)= 4$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1}(0) = p \\\\ x_{2}^{′} = f(t),\\end{vmatrix}x_{2}(0) = q$\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$, $x_2(t) = y'(t)$, and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate $(p-q)\\cdot f(t)$." 13194139535789 false] [17592186166633 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= y \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= y^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= x_{1}(0) = 1 \\\\\ny^{′}(0) &= x_{2}(0) = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′}(t) &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′}(t) &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' =-y' + 6y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' =-x_2 + 6x_1\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1}(0) = 1 \\\\ x_{2}^{′} = 6x_{1} - x_{2},\\end{vmatrix}x_{2}(0) = 4\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\np=1, q=4, f(t) = 6x_1 - x_2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(p - q) \\cdot f(t) &= (1 - 4)(6x_{1} - x_{2}) \\\\\n&=-3(6x_{1} - x_{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535789 false] [17592186166633 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = - y' + 6y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = - x_2 + 6x_1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=1, q=4, f(t) = 6x_1 - x_2,$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(1-4)(6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3(6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535789 true] [17592186166636 1100 "The second-order differential equation $y'' + 5y' - 2y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, where\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the matrix $A$." 13194139535789 false] [17592186166636 1100 "The second-order differential equation $y'' + 5y' - 2y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/assets/59e386be8b30fca475a2e0d164687a6561b9ab368b9879fc301d328896d184fd.png)\nFind the matrix $A.$" 13194139535789 true] [17592186166636 1145 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= y \\\\\nx_{2} &= y^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′} &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny''= -5y' + 2y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = -5x_2 + 2x_1\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = 2x_{1} - 5x_{2}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′} \\\\ x_{2}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535789 false] [17592186166636 1145 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny''= -5y' + 2y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = -5x_2 + 2x_1.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=2{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t)$ as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/assets/f35de5e6f066144c4130e38304abbff552730dc1fe97828572a66e68970c5d52.png)\n\nHence, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070402/assets/314f9dcf277b61dac9985c448aed98113516592e3822cc47a8566d28a480651b.png)" 13194139535789 true] [17592186166825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366308 13194139535789 true] [17592186166825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366315 13194139535789 true] [17592186166825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366322 13194139535789 true] [17592186166825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366329 13194139535789 true] [17592186166825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366336 13194139535789 true] [17592186166826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366343 13194139535789 true] [17592186166826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366350 13194139535789 true] [17592186166826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366357 13194139535789 true] [17592186166826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366364 13194139535789 true] [17592186166827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366371 13194139535789 true] [17592186166827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366378 13194139535789 true] [17592186166827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366385 13194139535789 true] [17592186166827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366392 13194139535789 true] [17592186166827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366399 13194139535789 true] [17592186166995 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535789 true] [17592186166995 1100 "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" 13194139535789 true] [17592186166995 1100 "The second-order initial value problem\n$y^{″} - 3y^{′} + 2y = 0, y(1) = 2, y^{′}(1) = 3$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1}(1) = p \\\\ x_{2}^{′} = f(t),\\end{vmatrix}x_{2}(1) = q$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$, and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate $(p - q) \\cdot f(t)$." 13194139535789 false] [17592186166995 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}-2y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=3,f(t)=-2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-3)f(t) \\\\  & =-f(t) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535789 true] [17592186366308 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366309 13194139535789 true] [17592186366308 1089 "q-109582" 13194139535789 true] [17592186366308 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535789 true] [17592186366308 1100 "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{′}-10y=0\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" 13194139535789 true] [17592186366308 1113 #uuid "95129f08-fcca-5b68-9041-afb0f8ffaa61" 13194139535789 true] [17592186366308 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{′}+10y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}+10{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=10{x}_{1}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=10{x}_{